hls: a video playlist needed anew starts at its segment's keyframe

A playlist that becomes needed during playback (another video variant
selected, with or without a seek after it) caught up with
AVSEEK_FLAG_ANY from the current position: its first packets were the
frames after the segment's keyframe. The same held for a needed video
playlist that is not the seek playlist (a seek on the audio stream).
For a playlist that is the seek playlist and becomes needed on the read
after the seek (a player sets the discards, then seeks), the catch-up
replaced the seek's keyed cutoff, which 0065 had relaxed by a segment,
with an unrelaxed one: the keyframe's DTS is below the EXTINF-derived
segment start whenever the timeline's first timestamp is a keyframe's
PTS, so it was dropped too. A software decoder hides the frames that
follow until the next keyframe (a GOP later, 6 s on some servers); a
hardware decoder fails on them (VideoToolbox: "co located POCs
unavailable").

A VOD playlist that brings a needed video stream now starts it at the
keyframe of the segment holding the position, as a backward seek on its
own video stream does: reading starts at that segment, the cutoff is a
segment early, and keyframes only. The caller gets up to a segment of
frames before the position, which it drops as after any seek. Without a
keyframe flag before the position (a segment that does not start with
one, an intra-refresh stream), the video starts at the position, as
before. The playlist's other streams (a muxed variant's audio) still
start at the position: up to the first of their packets there, or the
video past its segment, earlier ones are dropped, so audio does not go
back. Live playlists, and playlists without a video stream (audio
renditions, packed audio, subtitles), keep the AVSEEK_FLAG_ANY catch-up;
nothing changes without a seek or a newly needed playlist.

diff --git a/libavformat/hls.c b/libavformat/hls.c
--- a/libavformat/hls.c
